d","record_id":"case:cases/ga51_2024__2023-09.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Myers v. Illiana Presbytery","year":2024,"disposition":"granted","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga51_2024__2023-09.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga51_2024__2023-09.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:26-2--case:cases/ga51_2024__2023-09.md:occ-4ce418ab40dc","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga51_2024__2023-09.html","repository_path":"cases/ga51_2024__2023-09.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":863},"excerpt":"Constitutional amendment process (See BCO 26-2). It seems, therefore, that the Decision's mandated procedure is no more than judicial fiat. Is the SJC's “intrinsic power” to interpolate alleged Constitutional gaps better or more binding than that of the lower…","sources":["case_markdown_text","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga51_2024__2023-09.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["elevation of censure","excommunication","deposition from office","indefinite suspension from sacraments","due process in church discipline","contumacy","incorrigibility","judicial process vs. parliamentary process","de novo trial","censure without additional process"],"standard_of_review":"not_stated","case_numbers":["2023-9"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"An elder appealed Presbytery’s elevation, without additional process, of suspensions from office and the Sacraments to deposition and excommunication. The SJC granted the appeal, reversed in whole, and remanded, restoring the prior suspensions. Elevation required a hearing on incorrigibility and contumacy—with a charge, evidence, plea, and defense—but not a full de novo trial; disagreement with the original verdict alone was insufficient. The SJC left unresolved deposition from office when excommunication is absent."}},{"id":"bco:26-2--case:cases/ga21_1993__1992-06.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga21_1993__1992-06.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Conrad et al. v. Central Carolina Presbytery. (Case #1)","year":1992,"disposition":"administrative","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga21_1993__1992-06.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga21_1993__1992-06.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:26-2--case:cases/ga21_1993__1992-06.md:occ-81031904ffc8","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga21_1993__1992-06.html","repository_path":"cases/ga21_1993__1992-06.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":164},"excerpt":"…of the PCA without the PCA courts voting in the prescribed manner (BCO 26-2). Prior to this decision there were four ways to remove an elder from the active duty of his office.","sources":["case_markdown_text"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga21_1993__1992-06.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["judicial procedure","complaint handling","presbytery jurisdiction","interim session authority","administrative remedy"],"standard_of_review":"not_stated","case_numbers":["1992-6"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"After a congregation voted to ask that all ruling elders' official relationships be dissolved without censure, the Session concurred and requested an interim Session. The SJC denied the elders' complaint, holding that BCO 24-6b extends by good and necessary deduction from one elder to multiple identified elders. Dissolution ended their service on that Session but did not divest them of office. Because both the congregation and Session requested it, BCO 13-9 and 15-2 authorized Presbytery to appoint an interim 